          }
        },
        {
          "id": "q1",
          "type": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "Where do the branches of the root locus **start** (at $K = 0$)?",
          "options": [
            "At the open-loop poles",
            "At the open-loop zeros",
            "At the origin",
            "At infinity"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Write $1 + KG = 0$ as $D(s) + KN(s) = 0$. At $K = 0$ this is $D(s) = 0$: the closed-loop poles equal the open-loop poles. As $K \\to \\infty$, $N(s) = 0$ dominates and the poles approach the zeros (or infinity)."
        },
        {
          "id": "i2",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "How many branches?",
          "body": "With $n$ open-loop poles and $m$ zeros there are $n$ branches. $m$ of them end at the zeros; the remaining $n - m$ go off to infinity along **asymptotes**. Because complex poles come in conjugate pairs, the locus is always symmetric about the real axis."
        },
